# TEMPLATE -- copy to theater-sso.sops.yaml, fill in, then:
#     task secrets:seal -- apps/secrets/theater-sso.sops.yaml
#
# Credentials for the five oauth2-proxy instances in apps/theater/sso.yaml, which
# put Keycloak in front of the *arr web UIs.
#
# ONE Secret for all five, because they share ONE Keycloak client. See the header
# of apps/theater/sso.yaml for why that is the right granularity: a per-app client
# would mean five client secrets to rotate and five sets of redirect URIs to keep
# in step, for no isolation benefit -- they all guard the same person's access to
# the same namespace.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE KEYCLOAK SIDE, WHICH IS NOT IN GIT AND CANNOT BE
#
# There is no realm-as-code in this repository -- no keycloak-config-cli, no
# Operator CRs, no realm JSON. The `homelab` realm and every client in it live
# only in Keycloak's Postgres, which is why platform/keycloak/backup.yaml calls
# that database the thing whose loss means rebuilding the realm by hand.
#
# So this is a manual procedure. Do it once, in the Keycloak admin UI at
# https://auth.lilalala.com, realm `homelab`:
#
# 1. Clients -> Create client
#      Client ID:            theater-sso
#      Client authentication: ON        (confidential -- it has a secret)
#      Authentication flow:  Standard flow ON, Direct access grants OFF
#
# 2. Valid redirect URIs -- one per protected host, EXACTLY these paths:
#      https://sonarr.lilalala.com/oauth2/callback
#      https://radarr.lilalala.com/oauth2/callback
#      https://lidarr.lilalala.com/oauth2/callback
#      https://prowlarr.lilalala.com/oauth2/callback
#      https://qbittorrent.lilalala.com/oauth2/callback
#
#    A wildcard would be shorter and is worse: `https://*.lilalala.com/*` lets any
#    subdomain -- including one you have not created yet -- complete this client's
#    login flow and receive its tokens.
#
# 3. Credentials tab -> copy the Client secret into `client-secret` below.
#
# 4. AUTHORIZATION IS BY GROUP, and this part is load-bearing:
#
#      Groups -> Create group -> `theater-admins`, and add yourself to it.
#
#      Clients -> theater-sso -> Client scopes -> theater-sso-dedicated
#        -> Add mapper -> By configuration -> Group Membership
#             Name:            groups
#             Token Claim Name: groups
#             Full group path:  ON
#             Add to ID token:  ON
#             Add to access token: ON
#
#    WHY NOT `--email-domain=*`: the same realm authenticates Immich, Mealie,
#    Paperless and Grafana, and family accounts for photo and media sharing belong
#    in it. Any-authenticated-user would hand every one of them Sonarr, Radarr and
#    qBittorrent. The group check is what keeps "can watch films" separate from
#    "can reconfigure the download client".
#
#    `Full group path: ON` is why apps/theater/sso.yaml passes
#    `--allowed-group=/theater-admins` with a LEADING SLASH. Turn it off and the
#    claim becomes `theater-admins`, the flag stops matching, and every login gets
#    a 403 that looks like a broken client.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE COOKIE SECRET IS NOT FROM KEYCLOAK. GENERATE IT:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32 | tr -- '+/' '-_'
#
# oauth2-proxy requires EXACTLY 16, 24 or 32 bytes -- it refuses to start
# otherwise, with a message about cookie secret length. The `tr` makes it
# URL-safe base64, which is what oauth2-proxy expects.
#
# All five instances share it deliberately: apps/theater/sso.yaml sets
# `--cookie-domain=.lilalala.com` so one login covers all five hosts. Different
# secrets per instance would mean five logins, which is the opposite of the point.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KEY NAMES ARE FIXED BY THE DEPLOYMENTS
#
# apps/theater/sso.yaml reads these with `valueFrom.secretKeyRef` into
# OAUTH2_PROXY_CLIENT_SECRET and OAUTH2_PROXY_COOKIE_SECRET, which is how
# oauth2-proxy takes them from the environment. Rename a key here and every proxy
# crashloops with a missing-flag error.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: theater-sso
  namespace: theater
type: Opaque
stringData:
  client-secret: PUT_THE_KEYCLOAK_THEATER_SSO_CLIENT_SECRET_HERE
  cookie-secret: PUT_A_FRESHLY_GENERATED_COOKIE_SECRET_HERE
